.TH EUCLID-CLI-EMM-SET-THREADS 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-emm-set-threads \- set the number of worker threads a module's processes run
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli
[\fB\-\-endpoint\fR \fIURL\fR]
.B emm set-threads
.BR \-\-module " " \fIMODULE\fR
.BR \-\-threads " " \fIN\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
Sets how many worker threads each of a module's processes serves
requests with.
.PP
This is the companion to
.BR euclid-cli-emm-set-instances (1),
and the two answer different questions: instances are how many processes
of a module the manager runs, threads are how many requests one of those
processes can be in the middle of.
.PP
It matters most for the modules whose busiest action deliberately waits.
.IR ees "'s " receive-events " and " eqs "'s " receive-messages
hold a thread for as long as the caller asked them to wait, so with too
few threads the requests that are not waiting queue behind the ones that
are. One thread is kept out of the waiting for exactly this reason, so
the configured figure is how many callers may wait at once, plus one.
.SH APPLYING THE CHANGE
A thread count is fixed when a process starts \- the threads are created
once, as the module builds its I/O context \- so unlike an instance
limit it cannot be applied to a process that is already running. The
manager therefore restarts the module's instances to put it into effect,
.B one per reconcile tick
(roughly one every five seconds), so the rest of the pool keeps serving
while it works through them.
.PP
A module running a single instance is briefly unavailable while that one
instance restarts. Raise its minimum with
.BR euclid-cli-emm-set-instances (1)
first if that matters.
.PP
The setting is persisted in the module repository and read by the module
process itself as it starts, taking precedence over
.I euclid.modules.<module>.threads
in
.IR euclid.json .
It therefore survives a manager restart and a package upgrade, and does
not have to be applied to every host's configuration file. A module that
cannot reach the database as it starts falls back to the configuration
file rather than refusing to start.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-module " " \fIMODULE\fR
Module to set the thread count for, e.g. \fIeqs\fR. Required. Must be a
module the manager knows about \- an unknown name is rejected with
.I "HTTP 404"
rather than silently recorded.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-threads " " \fIN\fR
Number of worker threads each instance runs, between 1 and 256.
Required. One is the floor because a module with no thread answers
nothing; the ceiling is there because this is a typo away from a number
of OS threads that will not be created. A value outside the range is
refused rather than quietly corrected.
.SH OUTPUT
The JSON response is printed to standard output:
.RS
.TP
.I name
The module the thread count was set for.
.TP
.I threads
The count now in force.
.TP
.I runningInstances
How many instances the manager still has to cycle through before every
one of them is running with the new count.
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The thread count was accepted and recorded.
.TP
.B 1
The stored session is missing or not an administrator's, the module is
unknown, the count is out of range, the request could not be sent, or
the server rejected it. Details are printed to standard error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Give each EQS instance sixteen worker threads, so more callers can
long-poll for messages at once:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 emm set-threads \e
    --module eqs --threads 16
.fi
.RE
.PP
The same for EES, whose \fIreceive-events\fR holds a thread per waiting
subscriber:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 emm set-threads \e
    --module ees --threads 16
.fi
.RE
.PP
Make sure ESM has more than one instance before changing its threads, so
the restart is not an outage:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 emm set-instances \e
    --module esm --min 2

euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 emm set-threads \e
    --module esm --threads 8
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-emm (1),
.BR euclid-cli-emm-set-instances (1),
.BR euclid-cli-emm-list-modules (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-login (1)
